s To Work For \u2013 Southeast Asia", "year": 2025, "rank": 92, "itemDescription": "<p>Coca-Cola ranks No. 92 on the 2025 Best Companies to Work For Southeast Asia.</p>"}, {"slug": "aiq", "title": "Fortune AIQ 50", "year": 2025, "rank": 6, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 323, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 97, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 45, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 12, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 332, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--food-beverages-tobacco", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Food, Beverages, and Tobacco", "year": 2024, "rank": 4, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 95, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 28, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 15, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 15, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 344, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 109, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 100, "itemDescription": "The Coca-Cola Company owns a roster of over 200 brands that includes its eponymous soft drink; Powerade; Fairlife; and Bodyarmor, a recent acquisition.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>In 2022 the Coca-Cola Company had strong topline performance with $43 billion in revenues, an 11% increase compared to the prior year, but struggled to grow its profits; its $9.5 billion total represented a 2% decline. The company dropped seven spots on the Fortune 500.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Coca-Cola expanded into the alcoholic beverage market in the U.S. with the launch of a canned Jack Daniels bourbon and Coke drink after a successful 2022 pilot in Mexico.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>In March 2023 Coca-Cola inked a deal for an undisclosed sum with OpenAI for use of its ChatGPT and DALL-E chatbots. Details are still vague, but the company has already announced an advertising campaign in which it invites people \u201cto generate original artwork with iconic creative assets from the Coca-Cola archives.\u201d</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": 26, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 359, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 93, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">After a rough 2020, Coca-Cola\u2019s business recovered in 2021 as restrictions on public gatherings loosened across the U.S. and globally. One positive sign: In the fourth quarter, the company\u2019s away-from-home sales volume\u2014think beverages at concerts, restaurants, and sporting events\u2014finally caught up to pre-pandemic levels. For the full year, revenues increased to $38.7 billion, up 17% from 2020. A potential future growth driver: the company\u2019s late-2021 acquisition of Bodyarmor, a popular sports drink brand in which Coca-Cola previously owned a minority stake.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": 22, "itemDescription": "&nbsp;"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 370, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The pandemic battered Coca-Cola\u2019s business, with revenue falling 11.4% to $33 billion in 2020. Consumer cravings for soft drinks and snacks at home failed to match the corresponding cratering of demand for Coke products in restaurants, movie theaters, and other public destinations, where the company traditionally makes about half its sales. Amid these societal changes, the Atlanta-based beverage company made some changes of its own; Coca-Cola in August 2020 announced plans to restructure its workforce to focus on its fastest-growing products (Tab and Odwalla drinks got the ax). That plan meant cutting more than 2,000 jobs through layoffs and buyouts out of an employee base of just over 80,000.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The pandemic battered Coca-Cola\u2019s business, with revenue falling 11.4% to $33 billion in 2020. Consumer cravings for soft drinks and snacks at home failed to match the corresponding cratering of demand for Coke products in restaurants, movie theaters, and other public destinations, where the company traditionally makes about half its sales. Amid these societal changes, the Atlanta-based beverage company made some changes of its own; Coca-Cola in August 2020 announced plans to restructure its workforce to focus on its fastest-growing products (Tab and Odwalla drinks got the ax). That plan meant cutting more than 2,000 jobs through layoffs and buyouts out of an employee base of just over 80,000.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 93, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The pandemic battered Coca-Cola\u2019s business, with revenue falling 11.4% to $33 billion in 2020. Consumer cravings for soft drinks and snacks at home failed to match the corresponding cratering of demand for Coke products in restaurants, movie theaters, and other public destinations, where the company traditionally makes about half its sales. Amid these societal changes, the Atlanta-based beverage company made some changes of its own; Coca-Cola in August 2020 announced plans to restructure its workforce to focus on its fastest-growing products (Tab and Odwalla drinks got the ax). That plan meant cutting more than 2,000 jobs through layoffs and buyouts out of an employee base of just over 80,000.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The pandemic battered Coca-Cola\u2019s business, with revenue falling 11.4% to $33 billion in 2020. Consumer cravings for soft drinks and snacks at home failed to match the corresponding cratering of demand for Coke products in restaurants, movie theaters, and other public destinations, where the company traditionally makes about half its sales. Amid these societal changes, the Atlanta-based beverage company made some changes of its own; Coca-Cola in August 2020 announced plans to restructure its workforce to focus on its fastest-growing products (Tab and Odwalla drinks got the ax). That plan meant cutting more than 2,000 jobs through layoffs and buyouts out of an employee base of just over 80,000.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": 12, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 335, "itemDescription": "Coca-Cola\u2019s $4.9 billion acquisition of the coffee chain Costa\u2014the largest brand acquisition in its history, finalized in January 2019\u2014had a major hand in shaping its performance last year. The buy helped the 134-year-old soft drink company rocket 60 spots up the Global 500, gain an expanded retail footprint, grow to 23,600 employees, and bring in $37.2 billion in revenue\u2014a 17% rise over 2018. But more recent news has been less upbeat. Consumer stockpiling of water and sports drinks ahead of the coronavirus lockdown hasn't outweighed the effects of pandemic-related movie theater and restaurant closures on Coca-Cola's sales and profits. The company withdrew its 2020 outlook in March."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 88, "itemDescription": "Coca-Cola\u2019s $4.9 billion acquisition of the coffee chain Costa\u2014the largest brand acquisition in its history, announced in 2018 and finalized in January 2019\u2014had a major hand in shaping its performance last year. The buy helped the 134-year-old soft drink company rocket 12 spots up the <em>Fortune</em> 500, gain an expanded retail footprint, grow to 23,600 employees, and bring in $37.2 billion in revenue\u2014a 17% rise over 2018. But more recent news has been less upbeat. Consumer stockpiling of water and sports drinks ahead of the coronavirus lockdown hasn't outweighed the effects of pandemic-related movie theater and restaurant closures on Coca-Cola's sales and profits. The company withdrew its 2020 outlook in March."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": 15, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 395, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 100, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": 18, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 328, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 87, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-ad